---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ImagesToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/ImagesToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="images-to-pdf">
  <ImagesToPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to combine images into one PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in any mix of JPG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, GIF or BMP files. Your device decodes them as they arrive.</li>
      <li>Reorder the pages: drag a card, or use its arrow buttons, which work with the keyboard.</li>
      <li>Remove anything you do not want with the × button on the card.</li>
      <li>Choose the page layout: Auto for borderless pages that match each image, or a fixed size with a margin for printing.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create PDF</strong> and download. Your images stay loaded, so you can build again with different settings.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How each format is handled</h2>
    <p>
      The PDF specification defines only two image types that match everyday web
      formats: JPEG (DCTDecode) and PNG's underlying zlib stream (FlateDecode).
      All other formats must be converted before they go into a document. The table
      shows the path for each format.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Input</th>
            <th>Path into the PDF</th>
            <th>Quality</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>JPG / JPEG</td>
            <td>Embedded byte-for-byte</td>
            <td>Identical to the original, no re-encode</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PNG</td>
            <td>Embedded byte-for-byte</td>
            <td>Identical to the original, lossless</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>WebP</td>
            <td>Decoded in the browser, stored as PNG</td>
            <td>Lossless relative to the decoded pixels</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AVIF</td>
            <td>Decoded in the browser, stored as PNG</td>
            <td>Lossless relative to the decoded pixels; needs a browser with AVIF support</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>GIF</td>
            <td>First frame decoded, stored as PNG</td>
            <td>Lossless; animation is not preserved</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>BMP</td>
            <td>Decoded in the browser, stored as PNG</td>
            <td>Lossless, and usually much smaller than the source</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      WebP stored as PNG can be larger than the original file, because WebP is a
      much more modern compressor. This is necessary, because a PDF reader from 2010
      must be able to open the result.
    </p>

    <h2>Auto page size versus a fixed size</h2>
    <p>
      With <strong>Auto</strong>, each page has the exact pixel dimensions of its
      image, as PDF points. Nothing is scaled or cropped and there are no borders.
      This suits screenshots, artwork and comics, but the pages can all have
      different shapes.
    </p>
    <p>
      With a fixed size such as A4, all pages have the same dimensions and the
      images go inside your margin. Use this for a printer, a binder or a document
      management system. Leave orientation on Auto and each page rotates to suit
      its image, so a landscape photo is not letterboxed on a portrait page.
    </p>

    <h2>Photo orientation is handled for you</h2>
    <p>
      Cameras usually store photos in the sensor's native orientation and add an
      EXIF tag that says how to rotate them for display. PDF has no such tag, so a
      naive converter makes sideways pages. This tool reads the orientation tag and
      applies the rotation to the embedded image, so portrait phone photos stay
      portrait.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing is uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      Decoding uses the browser's own image pipeline, and pdf-lib builds the
      document in this tab. The tool also works offline. Related tools:
      <a href="/jpg-to-pdf">JPG to PDF</a>,
      <a href="/png-to-pdf">PNG to PDF</a> and
      <a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a> for the reverse direction.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
